	affiliations = {Department of Zoology, Faculty of Sciences, University of Granada, Granada, Spain; Institute for Resources, Environment and Sustainability, University of British Colombia, Vancouver, BC, Canada; Department of Environmental Science, Policy, and Management, University of California-Berkeley, Berkeley, United States},
	abstract = {Urbanization influences how people interact with nature, modifying perceptions of nature's benefits (ecosystem services) or harms (ecosystem disservices or ED). In urban areas, wildlife-mediated ecosystem services remain understudied despite their importance for human well-being, particularly cultural ecosystem services (CES). The provision of species-mediated CES and ED also depends on the composition of wildlife communities, which can vary within cities. Urban areas can be developed in a gradient that goes from a scattered development (low-density housing with dispersed green spaces such as gardens) to a clumped development (high-density housing with large green spaces such as parks). Previous studies suggest that species composition, including CES and ED providers, may shift along this gradient. Yet, it remains unclear whether variation in these services and disservices is driven by differences in human perception (i.e., perception hypothesis) or by changes in ecological provision (i.e., species assembly hypothesis). We investigated both hypotheses using a multidisciplinary approach combining 637 in-person surveys and bird censuses across three Mediterranean cities. Our study identified four key CES (identity, educational, inspirational and bequest), disservices, and their main service-providing species. CES and ED varied across the gradient (i.e. higher bequest in clumped development while higher disservices in scattered development), mainly due to differences in residents’ perceptions rather than changes in the underlying ecological provision. Perceptions were also shaped by socio-demographic factors. This information can help create conservation plans to enhance the perception of benefits and promoting CES-providing species, while minimizing the harms associated with urban birds. © 2026 The Authors},
	author_keywords = {Birds; Clumped development; Human perception; Land-sharing; Land-sparing; Scattered development},
	keywords = {bird; conservation planning; ecosystem service; greenspace; perception; residential development},
