	affiliations = {Interior Design, Telkom University, Bandung, Indonesia; Design for Performance and Interaction, University College London, London, United Kingdom},
	abstract = {Curiosity is a driving force that propels humans to interact and seek the unknown continually. In artworks, curiosity often triggers a break from routine, leading to deeper exploration. This deviation suggests that curiosity allows individuals to move beyond habitual patterns of perception and behaviour. Rather than distracting, curiosity prompts a natural desire to explore the artwork’s offerings. In interactive installations, curiosity is a vital catalyst for audience engagement. Elements like sudden changes in visuals or sound stimulate curiosity, encouraging the audience to explore how the artwork functions and what it represents. This engagement positions viewers as co-creators of the artistic experience, fostering a deep, dynamic interaction between audiences and the artwork. The three experiments outlined in this paper will demonstrate how shadows and sound in installations create immersive experiences, grab attention, and stimulate curiosity. Shadows, in their incomplete, ambiguous and uncanny nature combined with sound to reinforce visual elements, can foster exploration and social engagement. This paper proposes that forming a nodal spatial network for interactive installations can ignite curiosity, transforming everyday environments into memorable, personalised experiences. By integrating immersive shadow and sound techniques, urban spaces can be revitalised, turning monotonous settings into dynamic, curiosity-driven journeys. © 2025 Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group.},
