	affiliations = {Liberal Arts University, University for Humani¬ties, Yekaterinburg, Russian Federation},
	abstract = {The article describes the design of a new, multifac- eted measure to assess the psychological well-be- ing of city residents. This concept encapsulates a structured perception of the urban milieu, rooted in prior positive experiences, which are regarded as dependable and enduring. This perspective gen¬erally fosters a positive and assured outlook on the individual's future within this city. The article outlines the process of developing and verifying a methodology designed to evaluate the pronounce¬ment of psychological security components among urban dwellers. The significance of this approach is substantiated for conducting research in the field of social psychology, human ecology, and behav¬ioral geography. Moreover, it is crucial for compre¬hending the nature of urban dwellers' well-being, which is essential for the professional practices of architects and landscape architects. The study in¬volved 2128 participants aged between 18 and 64 years old (M = 29.54, SD = 8.82). Of these, 63% were women and 37% were men. The methodol¬ogy comprises 38 statements, which are measured on a five-point scale and belong to six subscales: "Freedom", "Comfort", "Self-efficacy", "Trusting relationships", "Control over the environment" h "Reliability. The developed diagnostic tool exhibits strong psychometric characteristics, such as retest reliability and consistency, and has been validated through comparison with the "Express Diagnostics of Personal Psychological Security" method developed by I.I. Prikhodko, the Psychological Security Index by S. Bogomaz and A. Gladkikh, which is based on R. Yanov-Bulman?s Basic Beliefs Scale, and the Psychological Security Scale in the city by J. Wkng and colleagues. The elaborated methodologi¬cal framework can be proposed for use in research, educational, and remedial programs, as well as for assessing the individual traits of psychological well- being among urban dwellers. The discovered met¬rics for residents' psychological well-being can be employed in future research, as their understanding provides a wide range of possibilities for tackling is¬sues related to mental health, wellness, and positive personal development in urban settings. © 2025 Rossiiskaya Akademiya Obrazovaniya. All rights reserved.},
