	affiliations = {College of Civil and Architectural Engineering, Chuzhou University, Chuzhou, China},
	abstract = {This study proposed a hypothesis to investigate the relationship between place attachment, restorative perception, and health benefits of recreationists in urban scenic areas. A structural relationship model was constructed between the three, and the Langya Mountain Scenic Area in China was used as an example to test the model. The results show that there are two paths for recreationists to assess the health benefits of the scenic environment: “place dependence → place identity → restorative perception → health benefits” and “place dependence → restorative perception → health benefits.” It indicates that placing attachment recreationists in the external environment enhances restorative perception and, thus, health benefits evaluation. This study fills a gap in the existing literature by exploring the interrelationships between place attachment, restorative perception, and health benefits. Previous studies have explored these factors individually, particularly in the context of urban scenic areas within China. Clarifying this complex psychological process provides a new perspective for understanding people’s emotional perception of urban scenic areas and the resulting physiological and psychological benefits. However, since this study focused on health benefits, further research is required on a broader range of potential benefits, such as social well-being and long-term health outcomes. © 2026 Taylor & Francis Group, LLC.},
