	doi = {10.1080/13504509.2023.2290180},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85179967417?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Graduate School of Techno-Design, Kookmin University, Seoul Special City, South Korea; School of Civil Engineering and Architecture, Zhejiang Sci-Tech University, Hangzhou, China; Department of Agricultural Production and Resource Economics, Technical University of Munich, Freising, Germany; Research Center for Eco-Environmental Sciences, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing, China; School of Economics and Management, Zhejiang Sci-Tech University, Hangzhou, China},
	abstract = {The global spread of the COVID-19 pandemic has brought vast challenges. The lifestyle of residents has been extended from long-term home isolation to outdoor public activities along the periphery of the residential area. In this context, residents have an increasing demand for urban green space (UGS). To achieve a comprehensive perception of landscapes and explore the influencing factors during pandemic, the study administered a survey to assess residents’ perceived demand for UGSs in April 2020. The 828 questionnaires were to explore respondents’ perceptions underlying the pressure from COVID-19, focusing on the physical-mental response, activity preferences, and overall satisfaction with UGSs. The study found three aspects of experiencing nature, rest and relaxation, and self-improvement are the key factors improving the residents’ long-term stay in UGS and the choice of venues for activities. The four categories of rest, sports, entertainment, and watching UGS are the main factors affecting residents’ long-term choices and choices of venues. The main categories of people activities are sports, watching, entertainment, and rest. Residents who went to UGS for recreation did not change their psychological health but it could regulate people’s emotions and have a positive emotional response effect. The three types of activities–rest, sports, and watching–are the main factors affecting the health of residents in UGS, and the impact of rest is the most important. The critical factors affecting satisfaction are ecological greening facilities, sanitation facilities, infrastructure, amusement, and entertainment facilities. This study could provide a health-activity-satisfaction evaluation for UGS design after the outbreak of the pandemic. © 2023 Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group.},
	author_keywords = {landscape perception; resident needs; satisfaction evaluation; Stress of COVID-19; urban green space},
