	abstract = {A knowledge gap in the cognitive processes regarding socioeconomic variables and younger people exists in relation to different elements of nature. This study aims to analyze young people’s knowledge and perception about a dry forest, and to associate their knowledge with socioeconomic variables. The first stage of the research consisted of collecting socioeconomic data from 65 student participants, then, using the free-list technique, we captured students’ knowledge of the fauna and flora of the Caatinga ecosystem. Lastly, we encouraged students to write essays about the Caatinga. A total of 106 animals and 82 plants were mentioned, and younger students from rural and urban areas had similar knowledge. However, among rural students, there was a greater homogeneity. Student age did not significantly affect any of the response variables related to knowledge on the Caatinga, and males significantly knew more general, animal, and plant items than females. This variation of local ecological knowledge among the young students may be the result of the established relationships and adaptive processes of the culture with the environment. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2023.},
