	affiliations = {Centre for Environmental Sciences, Research Group Zoology: Biodiversity and Toxicology, Hasselt University, Diepenbeek, Belgium; Department of Botany and Zoology, Faculty of Science, Masaryk University, Brno, Czech Republic; Department of Vaccinology, Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences, University of Antwerp, Antwerp, Belgium; Aquatic Biology, Interdisciplinary Research Facility Life Sciences, KU Leuven, Kortrijk, Belgium; Fundamental and Applied Research for Animals and Health (FARAH), Faculty of Veterinary Medicine, University of Liège, Liège, Belgium; Centre for General Practice, Department of Primary and Interdisciplinary Care, Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences, University of Antwerp, Antwerp, Belgium; Department of Environmental Sciences, Open University of the Netherlands, Heerlen, Netherlands; Biology Department, Vrije Universiteit Brussel, Brussels, Belgium; Centre for Environmental Sciences, Research Group Environmental Biology, Hasselt University, Diepenbeek, Belgium; Belgian Biodiversity Platform–Team Nature and Society, Research Institute Nature and Forest (INBO), Brussels, Belgium; Chair Care and the Natural Living Environment, Department of Primary and Interdisciplinary Care Antwerp (ELIZA), Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences, University of Antwerp, Antwerp, Belgium; Capacities for Biodiversity and Sustainable Development (CEBioS), Royal Belgian Institute for Natural Sciences, Brussels, Belgium},
	abstract = {Ebola and COVID-19 are textbook emerging diseases influenced by humans. Ebola is often considered a result of exotic nature threatening health. Conversely, COVID-19, emerged in an urban environment, entails risks worldwide. Geographical, virological and demographic differences influence risk perceptions and responses to both diseases. Because ecological understanding of urban human-animal relations improves disease risk assessment, we call for ethnographical exploration of this interface. ‘Global Urban Confinement Measures’ impact health by influencing disease perceptions, limiting nature access, and strengthening inequities. To prevent and mitigate zoonotic pandemics and their consequences, policy should promote nature connectedness, concert with stakeholders, and integrate nature-city-inhabitant interactions. © 2020 Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group.},
